my name is Lisa Snell and I'm the  director of education for the reason  foundation and today it's my great  pleasure to interview Andrew Campanella  who is the president of national school  choice week and we're just going to talk  a little bit about this historic moment  in school choice Andrew would you mind  telling us a little bit about national  school choice week and why school choice  is so important why school choice why do  you guys celebrate it sure well thank  you very much Lisa for having me thanks  to the folks of reason it's always good  to be here national school choice week  celebrates all types of effective  education options for kids from great  traditional public schools to public  charter schools magnet schools private  schools online learning and  homeschooling and we celebrate school  choice because we know that every single  child in this country is different and  every child deserves the opportunity to  go to a school that meets his or her  needs and interests and when we have  choice in everything else in our  American life we should be able to  choose the schools our kids go to so we  celebrate school choice where it exists  and that's millions of more people  choosing schools for their kids than  ever before but national school choice  week also provides a platform for folks  across the country and local communities  to say hey listen I want more choices  for my family can you tell us just a  little bit about the history of national  school choice leak how did you guys get  started where do you see it going you  know I think it's it's really a  testament to the hard work of so many  local organizations schools and  individuals across the country that  national school choice week started out  just a few years ago in 2011 there were  a hundred and fifty events all across  the country and we were incredibly  excited about that because it was the  first year and then we've just seen it  take off and grow because people saw the  positive focus that we put on school  choice during the week there's no  negativity there's no you know  backstabbing and you know any of the  internal warfare that sometimes you see  in the school choice movement is people  getting together coming together working  together for a common purpose and this  year there will be thousands of events  all across the country so let's switch  gears a little bit and talk a little bit  about school choice itself I know you've  been involved in the school choice  movement long  before you were part of school choice  week and you know we're kind of in this  moment of amazing growth you know we  have almost five percent of the public  students in America in charter schools  you know 2.5 million or something kids  what do you think is the most promising  thing about or talk a little bit about  the historical changes in school choice  I mean in say the year 2000 would we  have imagined that kids would have as  many options as they have now and maybe  a little bit about what do you think  some of the most innovative things that  are happening in the school choice  movement well I think you would have  thought in two thousand folks would have  said it would be great if we could get  so many folks not only choosing schools  for their kids and we have millions of  more families actively choosing schools  for their kids now than ever before but  so much demand for school choice and  we're seeing demand for school choice  increase every year what I think is  exciting is that you have growth in  different parts of the school choice  movement sort of growing exponentially  but on parallel tracks you see more  States doing open enrollment for  traditional public schools opening up  public options for families you see  growth in magnet schools they've been  around for a long time doing great work  we're seeing people adopt that model  more public charter schools there's a  waiting list of 1 million kids it's  enough to fill Yankee Stadium I think 25  times if you were to fill it with those  kids on the waiting lists scholarship  programs you seeing those programs grow  enormously every year homeschooling  grows at a rate of two percent online  learning is booming so what we have is  not just one sector of school choice  growing but all sectors so that's what  I'm most excited about is that we are  finally becoming a country where parents  are no longer conditioned to think that  they don't have choices when it comes to  their kids education but they're saying  hey I need a choice I deserve a choice  I've got a choice and everything else  now let's talk about the politics a  little bit and how that's really changed  you know school choice in some ways used  to be branded is kind of a conservative  issue and actually right now we've had  kind of a media thing going on between  Matt Damon and Jeb Bush right where Matt  Damon has been a proponent of public  schools but he sends his kids to private  school  jeb Bush's a proponent of all schools  and he actually sent some of his kids to  public schools can you talk just a  little bit about the how the politics of  school choice have changed over time  well first of all we are seeing more  folks on the left on the progressive  side of the aisle promotes school choice  than ever before whether it's open  enrollment or magnet schools charter  schools scholarship programs online  learning our homeschooling they are  increasingly saying listen this is the  way this is the catalytic reform that  can help all other reforms succeed while  helping kids right away get a better  education in terms of matt damon i think  he makes some good movies and i'll leave  it at that but what really does trouble  me about some of the politics is you  have folks in in New York City who are  you know the authors and antagonists  against school choice they're really the  most vocal people out there they didn't  send their kids to traditional public  schools and yet not only do they oppose  vouchers but they oppose open enrollment  in traditional public schools and I  really question you know what their  motives are because to me that's very  sad so on the one hand the politics  would you say have they improved over  time as far as bipartisanship or do you  think entrenched interest how much power  do entrenched interests still have you  know you're always going to have folks  who are in the bureaucracy and who don't  want change because change would upset  whatever it is they do for a living and  that's just not the same not just for  education reform and school choice but  for everything else but I think that  you're seeing more people cross the  aisle to support school choice than ever  before and the folks who oppose it are  not as widespread as they once were but  I think they're really centered in you  know places like New York frankly and  and you see people writing books about  school choice or going on TV and talking  negatively about it and it's really the  same few people over and over again as  opposed to a bunch of groups all over  the country because support has really  really grown and parents are winning  more battles and they're losing now so  would you say then that the support has  widened and the opposition is narrowed  yeah we found  and doing tracking polls and looking at  what people think that support for  school choice is growing the number of  people on the fence is shrinking but the  number of people who oppose it is  relatively remaining the same so what  you're seeing is people who are on the  fence overwhelmingly going to the side  of supporting school choice so as school  choice week continues year after year  and we're not promoting any specific  policy in fact a lot of folks who join  in with school choice we can't agree on  the specifics of all these different  policies but they agree on the basics  which is giving parents more power more  freedom to make good choices for their  kids we hope that even more people will  come off the fence support school choice  and even convince some of those skeptics  yeah I mean it does seem like when you  have examples of very organized efforts  against school choice like recently in  Louisiana right there was coming from  the president's administration an  organized effort to curtail the  Louisiana program and it does seem like  public outcry from across the board and  you have to look at an effort like that  and say not only what a terrible  situation that was but look at it and  say who actually came out and supported  that lawsuit you can't really find that  many people who were you know issuing  press releases holding rallies holding  events to say yeah let's sue to get rid  of a scholarship program that's helping  low-income kids go to great schools  you're not seeing that you might see  some snarky blog posts in favor of it  but that's all so let's just wrap up  with what do you think the biggest  challenge for the school choice movement  is and what do you think the future  holds the biggest challenge for the  school choice movement is continuing to  get along there's so many people who  support school choice in a variety of  different forms and it's easy to say  that just because you didn't agree with  one organization on one policy that  you're never going to talk to them again  but if folks want to advance educational  opportunity they need to see the forest  through the trees work together  collaborate together recognize that they  might not always get their way every  single time but a cohesive movement that  gets along and works together there's a  lot more effective than one that is  factionalized and fractured so that's  the biggest threat the biggest challenge  what do I think the future holds for  school choice I think the future holds  just absolute tremendous growth and so  many more opportunities for kids then we  could have imagined and you know we  can't write any of the choices off there  are so many great options within the  public system good options in the  private system online which could be  public or private and homeschooling  never forget the homeschoolers so to me  it's really one of the best parts of our  country right now is seeing people  finally making good choices because  they're able to make those good choices  for their kids when it comes to going to  where they'd send their kids to school
 

